n ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-2,$ since both denominators at $x=-2$ equal zero. So, $x=-2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=1,$ since both denominators at $x=1$ equal zero. So, $x=1$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342296" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342296" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{(x+4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-4$\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{x+4}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+4)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-4,$ since both denominators at $x=-4$ equal zero. So, $x=-4$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342275" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342295" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342303" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342479" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342367" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=0,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=0$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342299" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=9$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-9)(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-9)(x+1){y}^{″}+(x-9){y}^{′}+x(x+1)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x+1)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=-1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x-9=0$ or where $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is undefined. These occur at $x=9$ and $x\\le 0.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=9.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=-1,$ so is *not* analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ but the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9},\n$$\n\nis undefined about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=9,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)P(x)=(x-9)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x+1)},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=9,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-9)^{2}Q(x)=(x-9)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x-9}=(x-9)\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=9.$ Therefore, $x=9$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73