nswer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342644" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x+3)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ Thus, $x=-3$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{(x-1){e}^{x}}{x},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" 