athacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342435" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=4$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1)(x+2)(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+2}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=4.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=1,\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Thus, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)^{2}(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=4,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=(x-4)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342421" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=7$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=7.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}=\\frac{(x+1){e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=7,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-7)P(x)=(x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=7.$ Therefore, $x=7$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342438" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=6$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-6)(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=6,$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=-2,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x}{(x-6)(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Therefore, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=6,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)P(x)=(x-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=6,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}Q(x)=(x-6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x-6}{x(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=6.$ Therefore, $x=6$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9d5a40b41cd7c35c2649bce99a7377e8bdf4672a1b9f3e346072a18cee354131"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an ordinary"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "an irregular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "a regular singular"] ["text" "an irregular singular"] ["text" "an ordinary"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "q-342643" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "a regular singular"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/state.json" "file_sha256" "d96ae6a806cf04dca5a9d7bb5ecc226a3f18f0b8e6046fe73868b83e93421d76"} {"question" "e-21847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x-2)(x+3)y'' + (x+1)y' + xy = 0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n\n- $x=0$\n\n- $x=2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21847.json" "file_sha256" "93173ac90755f76df534769bf06aab113743a50f956cc28da9edcdc0bf9408b1"} {"question" "e-21847" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21847.json" "file_sha256" "93173ac90755f76df534769bf06aab113743a50f956cc28da9edcdc0bf9408b1"} {"question" "e-21848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n(x-5)y'' + \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}y' + \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^3}y = 0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}$ and $Q(x)=\\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The point $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21848.json" "file_sha256" "b4f7c98d2ad6af826ca0001a5bd0d1837ea2120ccaf9ef8d9f518f317b41068c"} {"question" "e-21848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5){y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{x-5}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{(x-5)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x) = (x-5) \\cdot \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} = \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=5$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21848.json" "file_sha256" "b4f7c98d2ad6af826ca0001a5bd0d1837ea2120ccaf9ef8d9f518f317b41068c"} {"question" "e-21849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21849.json" "file_sha256" "346c6deab5b068414ca8655fbe7bc98ab7e7f4fa8a6b275edd37812870c1e94f"} {"question" "e-21849" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x-2){y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(x-2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\nQ(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+1 = 0,$ which occurs at $x = -1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\nx^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061975/example-21849.json" "file_sha256" "346c6deab5b068414ca8655fbe7bc98ab7e7f4fa8a6b275edd37812870c1e94f"}] "usage" {"e-21849" [] "e-21848" [] "e-21847" []} "basis" 1992 "retractions" [[17592186124150 :question/problem "$(x-2)(x+3)y'' + (x+1)y' + xy = 0$\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n1. $x=-3$\n2. $x=0$\n3. $x=2$"] [17592186124150 :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 2)(x + 3)y^{″} + (x + 1)y^{′} + xy &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + (x + 1)/((x - 2)(x + 3))y^{′} + (x)/((x - 2)(x + 3))y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3$, since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0$. So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2$, since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186124151 :question/problem "$(x-5)y'' + \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}y' + \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^3}y = 0$\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. The functions $P(x)=\\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}$ and $Q(x)=\\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}$ are analytic about $x=5$.\n2. The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5$.\n3. The point $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation."] [17592186124151 :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 5)y^{″} + \\frac{x + 5}{x - 5}y^{′} + (3)/((x - 5)^{3})y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + (x + 5)/((x - 5)^{2})y^{′} + (3)/((x - 5)^{4})y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x) = \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5$, they are *not* analytic about $x=5$. So, statement I is false.\n- The function\n$(x-5)P(x) = (x-5) \\cdot \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} = \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}$\nis *not* analytic about $x=5$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=5$. Therefore, statement II is false.\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186124152 :question/problem "$x(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1$, $x = 0$, $x = 1$, and $x = 2$, indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular."] [17592186124152 :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a$. Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(x - 2)(x + 1)y^{″} + x(x - 2)y^{′} + (\\sin x)/((x - 2)^{2})y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + \\frac{1}{x + 1}y^{′} + (\\sin x)/(x(x + 1)(x - 2)^{3})y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1} \\quad\\text{and}\\quad Q(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where\n\n$$\nx+1 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich occurs at $x = -1$.\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where\n\n$$\nx(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich occurs at $x=-1$, $x=0$, and $x=2$.\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1$, the function\n$(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1$,\nis analytic about $x=-1$, and the function\n$(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3}$,\nis also analytic about $x=-1$. Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n- For $x=0$, the function\n$xP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1}$,\nis analytic about $x=0$, and the function\n$x^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3}$,\nis also analytic about $x=0$. Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n- For $x=2$, the function\n$(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1}$,\nis analytic about $x=2$, but the function\n$(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)}$,\nis *not* analytic about $x=2$. Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation."]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
